BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eN3qEohcommsWoRcQy16h
SUMMARY:Atomistic Spin Simulations and Magneto-Optical Studies of Two-Dimen
	sional van der Waals Magnetic Materials
DTSTAMP:20260730T054954Z
DTSTART:20260730T160000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Bharadwaj Peela  Masters Candidate  Thesis De
	fense  Department: Electrical and Computer Engineering  Location: Ralph O'
	Connor Building 300  Two-dimensional (2D) van der Waals magnetic materials
	 provide a unique platform for  investigating magnetism at the atomic scal
	e and offer promising opportunities for  next-generation spintronic and op
	to-spintronics technologies. Their weak interlayer  bonding\, reduced dime
	nsionality\, and highly tunable magnetic interactions enable the  explorat
	ion of both ultrafast nonequilibrium spin dynamics and equilibrium magneti
	c  phase transitions. Understanding these phenomena requires theoretical a
	pproaches capable  of connecting atomistic magnetic interactions to experi
	mentally observable behavior across  multiple timescales.  This thesis emp
	loys atomistic spin dynamics simulations to investigate magnetic  phenomen
	a in chromium-based van der Waals magnets under both dynamic and  equilibr
	ium conditions. The simulations are performed within the Landau–Lifshitz–G
	ilbert  formalism using the VAMPIRE atomistic spin simulation package\, en
	abling the  incorporation of exchange interactions\, magnetic anisotropy\,
	 external magnetic fields\, and  thermal fluctuations. Together with first
	-principles-derived magnetic parameters\, this  framework provides a quant
	itative description of spin dynamics and thermodynamic  magnetic propertie
	s.  The first part of this work examines ultrafast magnetization dynamics 
	in the van der  Waals ferromagnet Cr2Ge2Te6 driven by transient effective 
	magnetic fields generated  through chiral-phonon excitation in a neighbori
	ng CeF3 substrate. Time-dependent  atomistic spin simulations demonstrate 
	that these ultrafast fields induce helicity-dependent  magnetization dynam
	ics through rapid domain-wall motion\, reproducing experimentally  observe
	d demagnetization and recovery processes and providing microscopic insight
	 into  chiral-phonon-mediated magnetic proximity effects.  The second part
	 of this thesis investigates the magnetic ordering of self-intercalated  C
	r1+δTe2 compounds. Exchange interactions and magnetic anisotropies obtaine
	d from  density functional theory calculations are incorporated into atomi
	stic spin simulations to  predict equilibrium magnetization and Curie temp
	eratures for different intercalation  concentrations. The simulations esta
	blish the relationship between Cr intercalation\,  i  competing exchange i
	nteractions\, and the evolution of magnetic order\, demonstrating how  fir
	st-principles calculations can be combined with atomistic modeling to quan
	titatively  predict finite-temperature magnetic properties.  Together\, th
	ese studies demonstrate the versatility of atomistic spin simulations as a
	  multiscale computational framework for understanding both ultrafast spin
	 dynamics and  equilibrium magnetic phase transitions in two-dimensional v
	an der Waals materials. By  bridging first-principles calculations\, atomi
	stic modeling\, and magneto-optical experiments\,  this work provides fund
	amental insight into magnetic phenomena across disparate timescales  and c
	ontributes to the theoretical design of emerging spintronic materials and 
	devices.  &#160\;  (Department : Electrical and Computer Engineering)\n\nh
	ttps://events.rice.edu/event/435526-atomistic-spin-simulations-and-magneto
	-optical\n\nFrom Rice University - Open to the Public
URL:https://events.rice.edu/event/435526-atomistic-spin-simulations-and-mag
	neto-optical
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:Ralph O'Connor Building
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:rice-public-events
CATEGORIES:Rice University - Open to the Public
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
END:VEVENT
