UID:57thnW7dNJz7wbZ6_i82m
SUMMARY:Signal or Noise: Can Team Neurodynamics Add to the Understanding of
	 Teamwork Beyond Self-Report and Observation?
DTSTAMP:20260730T054954Z
DTSTART:20260804T150000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Maha Khalid  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis Defen
	se  Department: Psychology  Location: Sewall Hall 250  Progress in underst
	anding teamwork depends on progress in measuring it. Traditional approache
	s to assessing teamwork\, such as self-report and observational measures\,
	 provide valuable insight into team functioning. However\, many existing a
	pproaches capture static or retrospective accounts of teamwork and may not
	 fully reflect how teamwork evolves moment-to-moment. As a result\, there 
	is growing interest in methods capable of continuously capturing team dyna
	mics\, particularly in action teams\, which are characterized by time pres
	sure\, high stakes\, and rapidly changing task demands. One such approach\
	, team neurodynamics\, uses electroencephalography (EEG) to quantify patte
	rns of neurophysiological organization across team members during a task. 
	Although team neurodynamics has shown promise as a continuous measure of t
	eam functioning\, its value beyond established measures remains unclear. T
	he present study examined the incremental validity of team neurodynamics\,
	 using the EEG-derived Neurodynamic Information (NI) measure\, in predicti
	ng objective mission performance beyond traditional self-report and observ
	ational measures. Twenty four-person teams recruited from Rice University'
	s undergraduate and graduate student population participated in a simulate
	d military task developed in Unity to mimic a Marine Fire Support Team (Fi
	ST) mission. Teamwork was assessed using a condensed Team Diagnostic Surve
	y\, an event-based observational coding framework\, and team neurodynamics
	. Hierarchical multiple regression analyses evaluated the incremental vali
	dity of these measures. Predictors were entered sequentially from least re
	source-intensive to most: self-report\, observation\, and team neurodynami
	cs. The predictive utility of teamwork measures differed across the two ex
	perimental missions. During the more challenging mission\, self-report exp
	lained approximately 46% of the variance in objective mission performance\
	, and observation explained an additional 13% of the variance\, whereas no
	 significant predictors emerged during the less demanding mission. The tea
	m neurodynamic measure did not significantly predict objective mission per
	formance in either mission and did not explain additional variance beyond 
	the traditional teamwork measures. However\, in the more challenging missi
	on\, exploratory mediation analyses indicated that maximum NI\, representi
	ng the team's greatest degree of neurodynamic organization\, demonstrated 
	a statistically significant indirect relationship with objective mission p
	erformance through self-report. These findings support the utility of self
	-report and observational measures for predicting objective mission perfor
	mance under more demanding task conditions while providing preliminary evi
	dence suggesting that periods of peak neurodynamic organization may be ass
	ociated with teams' subjective evaluations of their functioning. The explo
	ratory mediation findings build on the Interactive Team Cognition perspect
	ive\, suggesting that peak neurodynamic organization may be associated wit
	h the interactional processes through which teams coordinate their activit
	ies and subsequently evaluate their functioning. This work contributes to 
	understanding the complementary role of neurodynamic indicators alongside 
	established self-report and observational approaches.  &#160\;  (Departmen
	t : Psychology)\n\nhttps://events.rice.edu/event/435311-signal-or-noise-ca
	n-team-neurodynamics-add-to-the\n\nFrom Rice University - Open to the Publ
	ic
URL:https://events.rice.edu/event/435311-signal-or-noise-can-team-neurodyna
	mics-add-to-the
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:Sewall Hall
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:rice-public-events
CATEGORIES:Rice University - Open to the Public
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
END:VEVENT
